 {
  "id": "G362",
  "article_id": "gnews:d0f3f83296df884fae64921e",
  "title": "Can Micron Technology Become the New Nvidia?",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-07",
  "article_opening": "Micron Technology ( MU +4.26% ) has had a phenomenal 2026 so far, rising around 240%, though it was up around 300% until a few days ago before artificial intelligence (AI)-centric stocks started to sell off. Regardless, Micron has had a great run, and it isn't looking to slow down anytime soon.\nMicron recently announced another jaw-dropping quarter with huge growth and incredible expectations for the following quarter, dropping huge news that tight market conditions could last until 2028. If tha",
  "passage": "…imbing the ladder toward Nvidia. However, it will likely never catch up, as Nvidia benefits from similar tailwinds to Micron.\nBut how close can it get?\nMicron could be a much larger company by this time next year\nBecause the memory chip market is cyclical, it is often difficult to assign it a regular valuation. This plays into the analysis, as Micron would fare far better if the market assigned it a normal premium. So, let's analyze Micron's stock in two ways. I'll assign it a 15x earnings premium and a 25x earnings premium to see what the range of outcomes could be.\nMicron operates on a non-standard fiscal year calendar, and its FY 2026 ends in August. So, I'll utilize FY 2027 projections. Wall Street analysts project earnings per share (EPS) of $149.64 next year, with the highest estimate at $221.27. At the midpoint, Micron's stock would trade between $2,244 and $3,741 per share, depending on whether it trades at 15 to 25 times earnings.\nThat equates to a market capitalization of about $2.5 trillion to $4.2 trillion -- not far off from Nvidia's current $4.7 trillion. Now, if the earnings come in at the high end of the projection and the stock trades for 25 times earnings, Micron's stock could be valued at $6.2 trillion -- a greater figure than Nvidia.\nHowever, this won't happen in a vacuum because Nvidia's stock will likely rise if Micron's does. I don't think Micron can catch Nvidia, but it still a great company to invest in .\nBy Keithen Drury – Jul 7, 2026 at 9:30AM EST",
  "statement": "Wall Street analysts project earnings per share (EPS) of $149.64 next year, with the highest estimate at $221.27."
 },
 {
  "id": "G227",
  "article_id": "gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6",
  "title": "Nvidia GPU Debt Backstop Unleashes the AI Project Trinity: Capital, Offtake and Datacenters",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-06",
  "article_opening": "Up until now the majority of AI buildouts have been primarily cashflow funded by the hyperscalers such as Google, Amazon, Meta, Microsoft, Oracle. Over the last year, that's started to turn with Oracle then Meta, and now even Google turning to debt. Nvidia revenue is skyrocketing, and even 3 years into the build out, the general market is still materially lower on shipment volumes and revenue estimates for Nvidia in the 2nd half of this year versus our through supply chain tracking in the Accele",
  "passage": "…e as ClusterMAX cannot be used for commercial purposes without our authorization.\nOur tokenomics practice tracks end token demand and consumption closely across hyperscalers and AI labs with a focus on SKU and model level ARRs for all key labs, model performance, token pricing, and more.\nLastly, our InferenceX benchmarking product continually measures and tracks real-world GPU inference throughput and token efficiency across inference systems, models and software configurations, enabling lenders and operators to quantify the productive output, and therefore the revenue-generating capacity, of a cluster they are financing. It is an important resource for mapping token demand into GPU demand.\nNvidia’s backstop program is only getting started, and we see announcements coming faster every quarter. Many Neoclouds are already deep in negotiations with Nvidia, and there are still others that are considering engaging with the backstop program. Let’s briefly discuss the deals that have been announced to date – all of which are in the Asia Pacific region so far.\nThe first is SharonAI’s 72MW AI factory in Australia. Announced in June 2026, the deal has SharonAI scaling up to as many as 40,000 GB300s under a six-year backstop. Sharon disclosed a total backstop value of $4.88B, which works out to an implied floor of about $2.33/hr/GPU on average over the six year period. Sharon AI’s footprint is now set to grow to 132MW with 102MW contracted, and the company guides to reaching more than 55,000 Nvidia GPUs in total (inclusive of the 40,000 GB300s backs…",
  "statement": "Nvidia’s backstop program is only getting started, and we see announcements coming faster every quarter. Many Neoclouds are already deep in negotiations with Nvidia"
 },
 {
  "id": "G337",
  "article_id": "gnews:4249e0578b11cedf5a71ff08",
  "title": "Maximize Spectral Efficiency with AI-Native RAN and NVIDIA AI Aerial",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-07",
  "article_opening": "By Cindy Goh\nNVIDIA AI Aerial enables an algorithms-first approach that shifts RAN design from fitting CPU compute budgets to maximizing spectral efficiency through AI-native Layer 1 and Layer 2 models.\nGPU acceleration unlocks higher-order spatial multiplexing, with ML-based beamforming delivering up to 1.62x throughput gains over traditional zero-forcing methods in 64T64R massive MIMO scenarios.\nDeep reinforcement learning link adaptation achieves 1.3x throughput improvement over outer-loop li",
  "passage": "…he compute burden sharply. NVIDIA analysis shows that in a 64T64R MU-MIMO scenario with 16 users and 2 layers per user, where users are randomly assigned SNRs in the [0-20] dB range, AI beamforming requires significantly more FLOPs than traditional regularized Zero Forcing (rZF), but this increase in compute produces a spectral efficiency gain of up to 1.62x higher throughput at 32 layers compared to the traditional zero-forcing beamforming algorithm.\nEven at a lower number of layers, ML-beamforming delivers 1.28x throughput improvement.\nGPU compute enables higher-quality beam weight generation at the scale and latency required for RAN deployment. This connects directly to field validation. SoftBank and NVIDIA recently reported stable outdoor 16-layer massive MU-MIMO operation on a GPU-based AI-RAN platform. The trial delivered roughly 3x the spectral efficiency of a conventional 4-layer baseline. GPU value is not abstract acceleration; it is the practical ability to sustain higher-order spatial multiplexing in a real system.\nLink adaptation is a complex MAC-layer control function. The scheduler must repeatedly pick a modulation and coding scheme (MCS) to maximize throughput while keeping block error rates (BLER) near targets for QOS and maximizing spectral efficiency under changing mobility and interference conditions.\nTraditional link adaptation schemes, such as outer-loop link adaptation (OLLA) implemented in vector engines in CPUs, are lightweight, reacting to feedback through hand-crafted…",
  "statement": "SoftBank and NVIDIA recently reported stable outdoor 16-layer massive MU-MIMO operation on a GPU-based AI-RAN platform"
 },
 {
  "id": "G143",
  "article_id": "gnews:c1658c3cc3b216fa2b0eaa54",
  "title": "Valve is bringing SteamOS to Nvidia GPUs, proving the Steam Machine was never the real plan",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-04",
  "article_opening": "By Tanveer Singh\nAfter a 7-year corporate stint, Tanveer found his love for writing and tech too much to resist. An MBA in Marketing and the owner of a PC building business, he writes on PC hardware, technology, and Windows. When not scouring the web for ideas, he can be found building PCs, watching anime, or playing Smash Karts on his RTX 3080 (sigh).\nThe Steam Machine was helpless against the dark times we're experiencing in the PC hardware market. Launching at a price that won't do any favors",
  "passage": "…ows.\nBy Samarveer Singh\nValve confirms that official Nvidia support is coming soon\nThe company is working closely with Nvidia\nRunning SteamOS on AMD hardware has always been way more seamless, while Nvidia GPUs have felt left out. Nvidia's proprietary drivers still don't gel well with the SteamOS environment, which has been designed around AMD's RDNA-based silicon. The open-source Mesa drivers for AMD GPUs are built into SteamOS, and even compatibility layers like Proton focus on AMD hardware. This is why Nvidia users face an uphill task when trying to get SteamOS to run as well on their machines, often foregoing SteamOS for alternative distros like Bazzite for better support.\nNow, however, Valve has confirmed that it has a growing team closely working with Team Green to bring official support for Nvidia GPUs. This comes from Valve engineer Pierre-Loup Griffais himself, who said in his interview with Verge that the company is working with AMD, Intel, and Nvidia to make the SteamOS experience better on a wider range of hardware. In his Bluesky post , he also said, \"If you have an AMD GPU, you can build your own Steam Machine now! More GPU support being worked on.\" With the SteamOS installation image updated to version 3.8.10 , Valve has expanded support to more AMD-powered handhelds and AMD discrete GPUs. The Nvidia rollout, however, will take time, as noted by Griffais. He doesn't expect anything before 2027, so Nvidia users have to wait, relying on Bazzite in the meantime.\nReleasing SteamOS fro…",
  "statement": "Valve has confirmed that it has a growing team closely working with Team Green to bring official support for Nvidia GPUs"
 },
 {
  "id": "G101",
  "article_id": "gnews:82efe7d37ce9286b38e5aac5",
  "title": "Nvidia’s Silent AI Colonialism Is Trapping East Asia in a Fossil-Fueled Hell - The Diplomat – Asia-Pacific Current Affairs Magazine",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-02",
  "article_opening": "Every new generation of microchips uses more energy, water, and toxic chemicals than the last – with Taiwan and South Korea bearing over 90 percent of the burden.\nBy Greenpeace East Asia and Alistair Alexander\nIn late May, during Nvidia’s CEO Jensen Huang’s visit to Taiwan, local activists from the environmental group Greenpeace intercepted him to deliver a five-layer model cake reading: “AI Needs Renewable Energy.” The unexpected stunt was a reference to Huang’s own recent industry remarks, whi",
  "passage": "…the company’s GPU shipments are forecast to grow at 26 percent per year.\nNvidia outsources most of its manufacturing to Taiwanese semiconductor maker TSMC. As every new generation of microchips uses more energy, water, and toxic chemicals than the last, and as the AI frenzy is driving unprecedented demands for more GPU semiconductors, the island has been left to deal with ballooning energy demands and emissions.\nTake Fab 25 as an example. This TSMC manufacturing center, to be built near Taichung city, will require at least 1 GW of power – roughly the same as the entire city of Taichung – and about 100,000 tonnes of water per day, roughly a fifth of the city’s daily water use. Across Taiwan, TSMC operates more than two dozen fabs, and is planning 10 more advanced fabs like Fab 25. As a result, TSMC alone could consume 24 percent of Taiwan’s electricity by 2030, up from over 12 percent last year.\nTo meet this surge, Taipower, Taiwan’s utility, plans to build 18 gas plants totaling about 18.2 GW, roughly 40 percent of Taiwan’s current generating capacity, largely to meet the demand of the semiconductor industry, led by TSMC. This will further lock Taiwan into fossil-fuel dependency, even as the island already relies on fossil fuels for more than 80 percent of its electricity.\nNear Taiwan, South Korea – another major Nvidia manufacturing hub – faces a similar challenge. Samsung and SK Hynix, the two local giants that produce most of Nvidia’s memory chips, are pursuing m…",
  "statement": "TSMC operates more than two dozen fabs, and is planning 10 more advanced fabs like Fab 25."
 },
 {
  "id": "G471",
  "article_id": "gnews:256eeb8951f1a92e56ea59ab",
  "title": "Nvidia Just Gave a $267 Billion Warning That Micron Stock Could Crash Before 2029 Is Over",
  "publication_day": "2026-09-01",
  "article_opening": "Over the last year, artificial intelligence (AI) memory stocks have been on fire. Expanding model sizes and more sophisticated agentic AI use cases are fueling a parallel boom between graphics processing units (GPUs) and the high-bandwidth memory (HBM) layered on top of these accelerators.\nWith its shares up by 697% over the last year, Micron Technology (NASDAQ: MU) has been one of the clearest beneficiaries of this memory boom. While the stock's parabolic ascent has been tough to ignore, some i",
  "passage": "…it until 2030 to assess whether the supply-and-demand dynamics have actually changed. With that said, markets also do not look three years ahead while earnings are still compounding, like they are for Micron. This is why the stock could continue to rise throughout 2027 and 2028 even though Nvidia's commitment table is already public knowledge. As of now, the memory supercycle still has years of contracted shipments locked in.\nFebruary 2029 is the moment Nvidia's low-commitment year moves inside the window where the market could actually change how it prices Micron. If Nvidia does in fact scale back its purchase orders, investors will stop applying a scarcity premium to the AI memory market. My prediction is that Micron stock could peak somewhere between December 2028 and January 2029 unless Nvidia ratchets up its commitments well ahead of fiscal 2030.\nUltimately, Nvidia's memory commitments -- and the lack of them after 2029 -- do not tell us for sure that the memory market will dry up by 2030. But unless Nvidia makes it explicit that it will need more HBM going into the next decade, and that it's paying shortage prices for that HBM, I'd expect a de-rating in Micron stock. More specifically, smart investors could start trimming their positions between December 2028 and January 2029, leaving the rest of the market holding the bag and fueling a sharper drawdown thereafter.",
  "statement": "My prediction is that Micron stock could peak somewhere between December 2028 and January 2029 unless Nvidia ratchets up its commitments well ahead of fiscal 2030."
 },
 {
  "id": "G335",
  "article_id": "gnews:b48593184704bbbe6eddbc03",
  "title": "As chip sector takes it on the chin, traders bet on a big Nvidia rally",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-07",
  "article_opening": "Is tech's biggest sleeping giant about to wake up?\nShares of Nvidia fought into the green despite a 5% sell-off in chip stocks tracked by the VanEck Semiconductor ETF (SMH) , a notable outperformance a day after a research report claiming the AI leader was at least a year behind on manufacturing of its next line of server racks.\nThe stock's trading just under $200, a level it's flirted with since late last month, down 17% from its May record and up just 4% on the year, as investors have turned t",
  "passage": "…ock's trading just under $200, a level it's flirted with since late last month, down 17% from its May record and up just 4% on the year, as investors have turned their attention to other AI components, like the memory-makers.\nOne thing Nvidia has those stocks don't right now: bullish options flows.\nMore than 1.5 million calls traded in Nvidia Tuesday, compared to under 690,000 puts, with more than twice as many calls bought versus puts bought, according to ThinkorSwim data. Volume ratios are about the opposite in the sector ETF, with puts outpacing calls almost four-to-one in SMH, with traders buying 33,000 puts, compared to just 7,300 calls.\nSimilar action took place in Nvidia Monday after the company disputed a report by SemiAnalysis that its next-gen Kyber server rack was running into delays. Calls more than doubled puts by volume with about two-thirds of the $600M of NVDA options premium tied to calls, and almost three times as many calls bought versus puts.\nOne group of trades that looked like they were initiated by a single trader bought a total $3.5 million of the 200-strike calls expiring at the end of July. Those contracts cost just under 7 bucks each at the time of the trade, meaning they still need about 5.5% more to pay off by month-end.\nIt looks like traders are hoping Nvidia's two-day firming will turn into a rally: as of writing the top five contracts are all calls expiring Wednesday. The most popular was the 200-strike, which traded almost 170,000 times for a total…",
  "statement": "the company disputed a report by SemiAnalysis that its next-gen Kyber server rack was running into delays"
 },
 {
  "id": "G316",
  "article_id": "gnews:f0d529209a91c7a237e117f1",
